Hey @wardpeet, hope you're doing well! Thank you so much for picking this up in #29760, and so quickly. I read through the tests and they cover every shape from the report: field level `$not` with several operators, top level `$not` with several fields, an operator map inside a top level `$not`, `$nor`, and `$and`/`$or` under `$not`, plus the nested and mixed cases I hadn't thought of. It's great that it follows the same approach pg and libsql got in #25353.

@DZH166, it's me again from the note above. Just a heads up that #29760 now covers this, so you don't end up duplicating work. If your regression tests have a case it doesn't hit yet, it could be really useful to share it there.

Thanks again everyone, and I'm glad to help test it if that would be useful.

issue.body:
### Please read this first

- **Have you read the docs?** [Agents SDK docs](https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-js/)
- **Have you searched for related issues?** Yes, I couldn't find one. The closest I saw was #361 (missing `groupId`), which is a different thing.

### Describe the bug

Hi there, hope you're all doing well! When one span in a batch can't go through `JSON.stringify`, `ConsoleSpanExporter` stops and never logs the spans after it in that batch.

Some context on where this lives: `ConsoleSpanExporter` is the default exporter when you use `@openai/agents-core` on its own, without `@openai/agents-openai`. It's created in [`defaultExporter()`](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/tracing/processor.ts#L471-L477), wrapped by the default `BatchTraceProcessor` in [`defaultProcessor()`](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/tracing/processor.ts#L479-L484), and registered in [`index.ts`](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/index.ts#L373). `@openai/agents` swaps it for the OpenAI exporter, so most people never see it.

To be upfront, this is pretty low severity. Redaction is on by default ([`config.ts`](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/config.ts#L76-L81)), and in that mode the exporter only prints a fixed "data is redacted" line. It only shows up once someone opts into data logging with `OPENAI_AGENTS_DONT_LOG_MODEL_DATA=0` and `OPENAI_AGENTS_DONT_LOG_TOOL_DATA=0`.

What happens today:

1. A batch holds a trace and three custom spans: `before`, `bad`, `after`. The `bad` span's data contains a cycle (or a `BigInt`).
2. `forceFlush()` calls `ConsoleSpanExporter.export()`, which loops over the items ([L85 to L101](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/tracing/processor.ts#L85-L101)) and logs each span with `JSON.stringify(item)` ([L99](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/tracing/processor.ts#L99)). For custom spans, `Span.toJSON()` keeps the nested `data` as is ([spans.ts L320](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/tracing/spans.ts#L320)).
3. The trace and `before` get logged.
4. `JSON.stringify` throws on `bad`, and since there's no try/catch inside the loop, the whole `export()` call rejects.
5. `BatchTraceProcessor` catches it and logs `Tracing exporter failed to export batch` ([L220 to L225](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/tracing/processor.ts#L220-L225)), and `forceFlush()` resolves normally.
6. `after` is never logged, and the caller has no way to know it was dropped.

### Debug information

- Agents SDK version: `@openai/agents-core` 0.21.0 (same code on main at 7589b9c)
- Related package versions (optional): none, `@openai/agents-core` only
- Runtime and version: Node.js v26.13.0
- Operating system: macOS 26.6.2
- Model and model provider: none needed
- Does the issue reproduce with the latest Agents SDK release? Yes
- Does the issue occur consistently or intermittently? Consistently

### Repro steps

Runs against the public API of `@openai/agents-core` with no API key needed. I ran it with `npx tsx repro.mts`.

```typescript
import {
  BatchTraceProcessor,
  ConsoleSpanExporter,
  createCustomSpan,
  setTraceProcessors,
  withTrace,
} from '@openai/agents-core';

process.env.OPENAI_AGENTS_DONT_LOG_MODEL_DATA = '0';
process.env.OPENAI_AGENTS_DONT_LOG_TOOL_DATA = '0';

const processor = new BatchTraceProcessor(new ConsoleSpanExporter());
setTraceProcessors([processor]);

await withTrace('repro', async () => {
  const before = createCustomSpan({ data: { name: 'before', data: {} } });
  before.start();
  before.end();

  const bad = createCustomSpan({ data: { name: 'bad', data: {} } });
  const cyclic: Record<string, unknown> = {};
  cyclic.self = cyclic;
  bad.spanData.data = cyclic;
  bad.start();
  bad.end();

  const after = createCustomSpan({ data: { name: 'after', data: {} } });
  after.start();
  after.end();
});

await processor.forceFlush();
console.log('forceFlush resolved');
```

Output (trimmed). Swapping `cyclic.self = cyclic` for `cyclic.big = 1n` gives `TypeError: Do not know how to serialize a BigInt` with the same result.

```text
[Exporter] Export trace traceId=trace_b898... name=repro
[Exporter] Export span: {"object":"trace.span",...,"span_data":{"type":"custom","name":"before","data":{}}}
Tracing exporter failed to export batch TypeError: Converting circular structure to JSON
    --> starting at object with constructor 'Object'
    --- property 'self' closes the circle
    at JSON.stringify (<anonymous>)
    at ConsoleSpanExporter.export (.../agents-core/src/tracing/processor.ts:99:53)
    at BatchTraceProcessor.forceFlush (.../agents-core/src/tracing/processor.ts:350:32)
forceFlush resolved
```

No `after` line anywhere.

### Expected behavior

The bad span gets skipped and the valid spans around it (`after` here) still get logged, with a warning that says how many were skipped.

This felt familiar because @seratch fixed the exact same failure for `OpenAITracingExporter` in #2277 ("export valid items when a sibling cannot be serialized"), which was a really nice fix. `ConsoleSpanExporter` looks like the sibling that didn't get the same treatment yet.

### A couple of questions

1. Would you be open to mirroring #2277 here? Something like a try/catch around each item inside the loop, counting skipped items, and one `logger.warn` at the end in the same `[non-fatal] Tracing: skipped N of M items during serialization.` style. Happy to put up a PR if that sounds right, or go another way if you'd prefer.
2. Something related I noticed while poking at this: with data logging on, if the bad data is passed when the span is created (instead of set afterwards like in my repro), it throws even earlier from the debug `JSON.stringify` in [`provider.ts` L327](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/tracing/provider.ts#L327), and the span is never created. Do you think that one is worth guarding too, or is it fine as is since it's only a debug log?

Thanks so much for taking a look, and any feedback or tips are very welcome!

### Bug Description

In Google Chat, when someone mentions a user whose display name starts with the bot's `userName` (like `@Bot Dev` when the bot is `Bot`), `onNewMention` fires even though the bot wasn't mentioned.

For context, `message.isMention` is what decides whether a message goes to `onNewMention`, and adapters like Slack, Discord and Telegram set it from platform data. The Google Chat adapter never sets it, so the core falls back to matching `@userName` in the text. That fallback can't tell `@Bot` apart from `@Bot Dev`.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Create a `Chat` with the gchat adapter, `userName: "Bot"` and `botUserId: "users/111"`.
2. A person posts `@Bot Dev can you look at this`. The message has one `USER_MENTION` annotation, for `users/222`.
3. `parseGoogleChatMessage` (webhook, `packages/adapter-gchat/src/index.ts:1442-1494`) and `parsePubSubMessage` (Pub/Sub, `:1247-1287`) both build the message without `isMention`.
4. `normalizeBotMentions` (`:2747-2790`) already compares each annotation's user with `botUserId` (`:2758`), but only uses that to rewrite the text.
5. Since `isMention` is undefined, `setMentionFlags` (`packages/chat/src/chat.ts:3535-3536`) calls `detectMention` (`:3626-3637`). Its pattern `(?<!\w)@Bot(?![\w-])` (case insensitive) matches the start of `@Bot Dev`.
6. `chat.ts:3479` routes the message to `onNewMention` with `isMention: true`.

### Expected Behavior

With `botUserId` set, only a `USER_MENTION` annotation whose user is `botUserId` should count as a mention. `@Bot Dev` (`users/222`) shouldn't fire `onNewMention`, and in subscribed threads `isMention` should be `false`.

The docs read that way too: `gchat.mdx:92` and `:159` say `botUserId` is needed for exact mention detection.

### Actual Behavior

`onNewMention` fires with `isMention: true`. Same result when `users/222` is another bot, and when `botUserId` is unset. A real `@Bot` annotation for `users/111` fires once, as it should.

Webhooks usually only deliver messages that mention the app, so I think the main exposure is the Pub/Sub path for subscribed spaces. There, `isMention` comes out wrong in subscribed threads too.

### Code Sample

```typescript
import { createGoogleChatAdapter } from "@chat-adapter/gchat";
import { createMemoryState } from "@chat-adapter/state-memory";
import { Chat } from "chat";

const gchat = createGoogleChatAdapter({
  credentials,
  userName: "Bot",
  botUserId: "users/111",
});
const chat = new Chat({
  userName: "Bot",
  adapters: { gchat },
  state: createMemoryState(),
});

chat.onNewMention(async (_thread, message) => {
  console.log("mention", message.text, message.isMention);
});

// incoming message
// text: "@Bot Dev can you look at this"
// annotations: [{
//   type: "USER_MENTION",
//   startIndex: 0,
//   length: 8,
//   userMention: {
//     user: { name: "users/222", displayName: "Bot Dev", type: "HUMAN" },
//     type: "MENTION",
//   },
// }]
//
// logs: mention "@Bot Dev can you look at this" true
```

I have this as a small vitest in `packages/integration-tests` using `createGoogleChatEvent` and `createGoogleChatWebhookRequest` from `gchat-utils`. Happy to share it if that helps.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["@Bot Dev ... annotation users/222"] --> B["gchat adapter: isMention unset"]
  B --> C["detectMention: /@Bot/ matches"]
  C --> D["onNewMention fires"]
```

### Chat SDK Version

4.44.0 (main at 9476152)

### Node.js Version

_No response_

### Platform Adapter

Google Chat

### Operating System

_No response_

### Additional Context

This looks like the same kind of thing @onmax fixed for Teams in #1078, which leans on #946 so an adapter's `false` sticks. Thanks Max, that PR made this one really easy to spot.

One way I could see fixing it: when `botUserId` is set, `isMention` is true if any annotation's user equals `botUserId`, otherwise `false`. When `botUserId` is unset, leave it undefined so the text fallback still runs, since `gchat.mdx:149-151` documents that fallback and the existing test at `gchat-utils.ts:52-66` / `gchat.test.ts:121` runs without `botUserId`.

A couple of things I wasn't sure about:

1. Does that direction sound right to you, or would you rather handle it somewhere else?
2. When `botUserId` is unset, should annotations still rule out mentions of human users? A `HUMAN` annotation can never be the bot, so it feels safe, but I didn't want to guess.

If it helps, I'd be glad to put up a PR mirroring #1078. Thanks for taking a look!

### Describe the bug

Hi! When a model is selected by a provider prefixed name like `openai/gpt-5.4`, the runner treats it as a legacy model and quietly drops the reasoning and verbosity settings.

Some context on what the code is for: [`isGpt5OrNewerReasoningModel`](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/defaultModel.ts#L28-L34) decides whether a model name belongs to the GPT-5 and newer family, and [`adjustModelSettingsForLegacyModel`](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/runner/modelSettings.ts#L67-L90) uses that to strip reasoning settings that older models would reject. That's a really sensible guard, it just doesn't recognize prefixed names.

What happens today:

1. A custom or gateway `ModelProvider` gets `model: 'openai/gpt-5.4'` as a string (`OpenAIProvider.getModel` also [passes names through as is](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-openai/src/openaiProvider.ts#L129)).
2. The runner keeps the raw string as `resolvedModelName` ([run.ts#L1180](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/run.ts#L1180)).
3. `isGpt5OrNewerReasoningModel` tests it against `/^gpt-(\d+).../`. Because of the `^` anchor, `openai/gpt-5.4` doesn't match, so it counts as legacy.
4. Since the default model (`gpt-5.6-luna`) is GPT-5 family, [run.ts#L3794](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/run.ts#L3794) goes down the strip path, which [removes](https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-js/blob/7589b9c40120b89a7d2d28a5a60b4defe1ed7bf5/packages/agents-core/src/runner/modelSettings.ts#L92-L135) `reasoning.effort`, `providerData.reasoning_effort` and `text.verbosity` with no warning.

The same anchored check also feeds `getDefaultModelSettings`, `isGpt5OrNewerDefault` and `getDefaultModel`. So with no settings given, `openai/gpt-5.4` gets `{}` instead of the GPT-5 defaults, and with `OPENAI_DEFAULT_MODEL=openai/gpt-5.4`, `isGpt5OrNewerDefault()` returns false and the default settings become `{}`.

Scope as far as I can tell: only string model names are affected (so mostly custom or gateway providers), not AI SDK `Model` objects. The Python SDK has the same anchored regex but never strips settings, so the silent drop seems to be JS only.

I noticed #2281 just made `openaiResponsesCompactionSession.ts` tolerant of this by checking after the last `/`, and #1849 / #1850 fixed the same kind of silent stripping for `gpt-6-astra`. Thanks for both, they made this one much easier to track down!

### Debug information

- Agents SDK version: 0.21.0 (main at 7589b9c)
- Related package versions (optional, e.g. `openai`, `zod`, or `@openai/agents-extensions`): n/a
- Runtime and version (e.g. Node.js, Deno, or Bun): Node.js v24.13.0, pnpm 12.6.0
- Operating system: macOS 26.6.2 (arm64)
- Model and model provider: `openai/gpt-5.4` through a custom `ModelProvider`
- Does the issue reproduce with the latest Agents SDK release? Yes
- Does the issue occur consistently or intermittently? Consistently

Settings the model actually received:

```text
gpt-5.4        -> {"reasoning":{"effort":"high"},"providerData":{"reasoning_effort":"high","text":{"verbosity":"high"}}}
openai/gpt-5.4 -> {"reasoning":{},"providerData":{"text":{}}}
```

### Repro steps

A fake model that just records the settings it receives:

```typescript
import {
  Agent,
  Runner,
  Usage,
  type Model,
  type ModelProvider,
  type ModelRequest,
} from "@openai/agents";

const seen: Record<string, unknown> = {};

class RecordingModel implements Model {
  constructor(private name: string) {}
  async getResponse(req: ModelRequest) {
    seen[this.name] = req.modelSettings;
    return {
      usage: new Usage(),
      output: [
        {
          type: "message",
          role: "assistant",
          status: "completed",
          content: [{ type: "output_text", text: "ok" }],
        },
      ],
    } as any;
  }
  getStreamedResponse(): any {
    throw new Error("unused");
  }
}

const provider: ModelProvider = {
  getModel: (name = "") => new RecordingModel(name),
};
const runner = new Runner({ modelProvider: provider });

for (const model of ["gpt-5.4", "openai/gpt-5.4"]) {
  const agent = new Agent({
    name: "repro",
    model,
    modelSettings: {
      reasoning: { effort: "high" },
      providerData: { reasoning_effort: "high", text: { verbosity: "high" } },
    },
  });
  await runner.run(agent, "hi");
}

console.log(JSON.stringify(seen, null, 2));
```

### Expected behavior

`openai/gpt-5.4` should be treated the same as `gpt-5.4`: settings pass through untouched, and the GPT-5 defaults apply when none are given.

I'd love your take on the fix. Would you prefer normalizing after the last `/` inside `isGpt5OrNewerReasoningModel` and friends, the way #2281 did, so every caller benefits? Or stripping the prefix once at the provider or runner level, so the model helpers only ever see bare names? Very open to a better option I'm not seeing. Either way, I'm happy to put up a PR if you'd like.

