comment.body:
Hi everyone, hope you're all doing well! First time chiming in here.

Thanks @sadra-barikbin for the really clear write up comparing the two formats, and @lhoestq for explaining the TGI `value` blocker, that made the history easy to follow.

I was looking into this and it seems like @Wauplin's #1537 may have covered it. Since `@huggingface/tasks` 0.19.19 (picked up by `@huggingface/inference` 4.1.0), `response_format` accepts `text`, `json_object` and `json_schema` with a nested `json_schema: { name, description?, schema?, strict? }`, and `value` is no longer required. This type checks for me on the latest release:

```ts
const out = await client.chatCompletion({ model, provider, messages,
  response_format: {
    type: "json_schema",
    json_schema: { name: "person", schema: { type: "object", properties: { name: { type: "string" } } } },
  },
});
```

Whether the schema really gets enforced probably still depends on the provider and model, so I may be missing something there.

@sadra-barikbin @Cristy94 does this work for you now? And @lhoestq @Wauplin, do you think this one can be closed, or is anything still missing? Any tips welcome, thanks!

Hey @thomas0369, hope you're doing well! Thanks for such a clear report. The per worker numbers and the ~100s drop pattern made this really easy to follow.

I had a look on current main (5c33d89) and I think the `keepAliveMs` note fits `createMcpHandler`, but maybe not `McpAgent.serve()` yet. Happy to be corrected if I'm misreading it:

- `serve()` only takes `binding`, `corsOptions`, `transport` and `jurisdiction`, so there's nowhere to pass `keepAliveMs`: [types.ts#L37-L42](https://github.com/cloudflare/agents/blob/5c33d89791d6a00c6ea64cf7790fa753e3e5cbfd/packages/agents/src/mcp/types.ts#L37-L42), [legacy-agent.ts#L573-L580](https://github.com/cloudflare/agents/blob/5c33d89791d6a00c6ea64cf7790fa753e3e5cbfd/packages/agents/src/mcp/server/legacy-agent.ts#L573-L580)
- The POST stream already gets a 25s keepalive through `startKeepalive` (really nice work from #1583): [utils.ts#L328](https://github.com/cloudflare/agents/blob/5c33d89791d6a00c6ea64cf7790fa753e3e5cbfd/packages/agents/src/mcp/server/utils.ts#L328)
- The standalone GET stream and the legacy SSE GET stream don't seem to start one, so an idle GET would still hit the 100s proxy timeout: [utils.ts#L391-L512](https://github.com/cloudflare/agents/blob/5c33d89791d6a00c6ea64cf7790fa753e3e5cbfd/packages/agents/src/mcp/server/utils.ts#L391-L512), [utils.ts#L594-L706](https://github.com/cloudflare/agents/blob/5c33d89791d6a00c6ea64cf7790fa753e3e5cbfd/packages/agents/src/mcp/server/utils.ts#L594-L706)

Something that might work today, if you don't need the McpAgent Durable Object state: `createMcpHandler` passes `keepAliveMs` through to the SDK transport, which defaults to 15s ([handler-stateless.ts#L180](https://github.com/cloudflare/agents/blob/5c33d89791d6a00c6ea64cf7790fa753e3e5cbfd/packages/agents/src/mcp/server/handler-stateless.ts#L180)):

```ts
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/server";
import { createMcpHandler } from "agents/mcp/server";

const handler = createMcpHandler(
  () => new McpServer({ name: "my-server", version: "1.0.0" }),
  { keepAliveMs: 25_000 }
);

export default { fetch: handler } satisfies ExportedHandler<Env>;
```

(I haven't run this end to end yet, so happy to be told if the v2 transport handles the GET stream differently.)

For a fix, I wonder if the simplest route is calling the existing `startKeepalive(writer, encoder)` on both GET streams and clearing it in the ws `close` and `error` listeners, the same way the POST path does.

@thomas0369 does that workaround fit your setup, or are you relying on McpAgent state? And for the maintainers, would it help if I filed this as a separate bug? Glad to help however is useful, and any tips are very welcome!

issue.body:
Hey everyone, hope you're doing well! First time opening something here, so hello 👋

Summary: the `fetch` you pass in `Options` is used for the first request of a call, but the queue polling and result downloads that come after it go through global `fetch`.

What it's for: `Options.fetch` is documented as "Custom fetch function to use instead of the default one, for example to use a proxy or edit headers" (`src/types.ts`). It works for the model lookup and the initial submit (`utils/request.ts`), and #2377 recently fixed the `provider: "auto"` case (thanks kurian-j, that was a nice catch).

Current behavior:
1. Call `textToVideo` (or `textToImage`) on a queue based provider like fal-ai with a custom `fetch`.
2. The model lookup and the submit go through the custom `fetch`.
3. The task calls `providerHelper.getResponse(response, url, headers, undefined, signal)`, which gets `signal` but no `fetch`.
4. The status polling and result requests then use global `fetch`, with the auth headers attached, and so does the download of the output file.

The same pattern shows up in the polling loops in fal-ai, together, novita, wavespeed and zai-org, and in the output downloads in replicate, hf-inference and those same providers.

Expected: every request in the call goes through the custom `fetch`, so a proxy or header change applies the whole way through.

Repro (global `fetch` stubbed, custom `fetch` records calls):

```ts
const log = (tag: string) => (url: RequestInfo | URL, init?: RequestInit) => {
  const auth = new Headers(init?.headers).has("Authorization") ? " [auth]" : "";
  console.log(tag, String(url) + auth);
  return fakeResponse(url);
};
globalThis.fetch = log("GLOBAL") as typeof fetch;

await new InferenceClient("hf_xxx").textToVideo(
  { model: "acme/vid", provider: "fal-ai", inputs: "a cat" },
  { fetch: log("CUSTOM") as typeof fetch }
);
```

(`fakeResponse` just returns a stub queue response, happy to share the full script)

```
CUSTOM https://huggingface.co/api/models/acme/vid?expand[]=inferenceProviderMapping [auth]
CUSTOM https://router.huggingface.co/fal-ai/fal-ai/vid?_subdomain=queue [auth]
GLOBAL https://router.huggingface.co/fal-ai/fal-ai/vid/requests/r1/status?_subdomain=queue [auth]
GLOBAL https://router.huggingface.co/fal-ai/fal-ai/vid/requests/r1?_subdomain=queue [auth]
GLOBAL https://cdn.example/out.mp4
```

How would you like this fixed? My first thought was to pass `fetch` into `getResponse` the same way `signal` was threaded through in #2112, then use `fetchFn ?? fetch` at each polling and download call (decoding `data:` URLs could stay as is). It touches roughly 30 call sites across providers, so I'd rather check before going ahead. I also saw #2759 (default model lookup) and #2380 (polling loops) are open, so tell me if you'd prefer this to wait or fit around those.

I'm happy to put up the PR if that sounds good. Any tips on how you like fixes like this split or scoped would be really appreciated. Thanks for all the work on this library!

