issue.body:
### Version

deepagents 1.16.2, `main` at e80b3af.

### Summary

When the parent makes two `task` calls in one step, a subagent that edited nothing can quietly put back the old copy of a file its sibling just edited.

### What this code is for

The `task` tool lets the parent hand work to subagents, and the prompt nudges the model to run independent ones together ("Launch multiple agents concurrently when their tasks are independent", [subagents.ts#L130](https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagentsjs/blob/e80b3af/libs/deepagents/src/middleware/subagents.ts#L130)). `fileDataReducer` was added in #74 so those parallel subagents can all write to `files` without the old LastValue crash ([fs.ts#L647](https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagentsjs/blob/e80b3af/libs/deepagents/src/middleware/fs.ts#L647)). That fix has been really useful, thank you for it.

### Current behavior

1. `files` is not in `EXCLUDED_STATE_KEYS` ([subagents.ts#L78-L90](https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagentsjs/blob/e80b3af/libs/deepagents/src/middleware/subagents.ts#L78-L90)), so `filterStateForSubagent` hands each subagent the parent's whole `files` map ([#L882-L884](https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagentsjs/blob/e80b3af/libs/deepagents/src/middleware/subagents.ts#L882-L884)).
2. When a subagent finishes, `returnCommandWithStateUpdate` sends its whole `files` map back as the update ([#L448-L488](https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagentsjs/blob/e80b3af/libs/deepagents/src/middleware/subagents.ts#L448-L488), called at [#L940](https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagentsjs/blob/e80b3af/libs/deepagents/src/middleware/subagents.ts#L940)), including files it never touched.
3. `fileDataReducer` applies the updates key by key, last one wins, with no check for whether the value changed ([fs.ts#L654-L684](https://github.com/langchain-ai/deepagentsjs/blob/e80b3af/libs/deepagents/src/middleware/fs.ts#L654-L684)).
4. So if the editing subagent's update lands first and the idle one lands second, the idle one writes the stale `/a.txt` back over the edit. No error, and the editor's tool message still says it edited the file.

Results from the repro below:

- editor then idle: `/a.txt` ends as `v1` (edit lost)
- idle then editor: `v2`
- editor alone: `v2`

Same thing with `write_file` instead of `edit_file`.

### Expected behavior

A subagent returns only the files it changed (or deleted), so a sibling's edit survives no matter which order the updates land in.

### Minimal repro

Dropped into `libs/deepagents/src/` and run with vitest:

```ts
import { it } from "vitest";
import { FakeListChatModel } from "@langchain/core/utils/testing";
import { AIMessage, HumanMessage } from "@langchain/core/messages";
import { createDeepAgent } from "./agent.js";
import { createFileData } from "./backends/utils.js";

class FakeModel extends FakeListChatModel {
  private i = 0;
  constructor(responses: (string | AIMessage)[]) {
    super({ responses: responses as string[] });
  }
  override bindTools(tools: any) {
    const bound = super.bindTools(tools) as any;
    const inner = bound.bound ?? bound;
    inner._currentResponse = () => this.responses[this.i];
    inner._incrementResponse = () => { this.i++; };
    return bound;
  }
}

const call = (name: string, args: object, id = name) =>
  new AIMessage({ content: "", tool_calls: [{ id, name, args }] });

async function run(order: string[]) {
  const agent = createDeepAgent({
    model: new FakeModel([
      new AIMessage({
        content: "",
        tool_calls: order.map((t, i) => ({ id: `t${i}`, name: "task", args: { description: t, subagent_type: t } })),
      }),
      "done",
    ]),
    subagents: [
      {
        name: "editor", description: "edits /a.txt", systemPrompt: "edit",
        model: new FakeModel([call("edit_file", { file_path: "/a.txt", old_string: "v1", new_string: "v2" }), "edited"]),
      },
      { name: "idle", description: "does nothing", systemPrompt: "noop", model: new FakeModel(["nothing to do"]) },
    ],
  });
  const result: any = await agent.invoke({
    messages: [new HumanMessage("go")],
    files: { "/a.txt": createFileData("v1") },
  } as any);
  console.log(order.join(" then "), "=>", result.files["/a.txt"].content);
}

it("parallel task calls", async () => {
  await run(["editor", "idle"]); // editor then idle => v1
  await run(["idle", "editor"]); // idle then editor => v2
  await run(["editor"]);         // editor => v2
});
```

(The small `FakeModel` wrapper is only there so the bound model steps through its responses.)

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant P as Parent
    participant E as editor
    participant I as idle
    participant R as fileDataReducer
    P->>E: task (files: /a.txt = v1)
    P->>I: task (files: /a.txt = v1)
    E->>E: edit_file v1 to v2
    E-->>R: files { /a.txt: v2 }
    I-->>R: files { /a.txt: v1 } (untouched copy)
    R->>P: /a.txt = v1, edit lost
```

### Question

What fix would you prefer here? One idea: diff the subagent's final `files` against the map it was given, and return only changed keys (plus `null` for deletions). That would also shrink what ends up in checkpoints, which I think is the same root cause as #990. For what it's worth, the Python side already drops unchanged keys via `_is_unchanged` in `subagents.py`, though it can still send stale copies of other files when one of them changes, so a diff per key seems closer to the goal. But maybe you'd rather go another way, like excluding `files` and having subagents send an explicit delta, or handling it in the reducer. Happy to put up a PR for whichever direction you like.

