Hey @Alex-Prevot, hope you're doing well! Thanks for digging into the biggest threads and sharing real numbers, that made this much easier to trace. Here's what I found on current main (e80b3af), happy to be corrected by the maintainers on any of it.

1. Yes, `files` goes to subagents today. It isn't in `EXCLUDED_STATE_KEYS` (`libs/deepagents/src/middleware/subagents.ts` L78 to L90), so `filterStateForSubagent` passes the whole map in (L882 to L884). `returnCommandWithStateUpdate` then sends the whole map back as the update (L448 to L488), not just what the subagent changed. With the default `StateBackend` that's how subagents see files the parent wrote. But returning the full map means each `task` call writes every file again, which fits the growth you're seeing. It can also undo a sibling's edit when subagents run in parallel, which I wrote up in #1036. Returning only the keys a subagent actually changed seems like it would fix both. Maintainers, would you be open to that?

2. I think you read `offloadBinaryContent` right on both points. It only replaces blocks whose `data` is a base64 string (`middleware/blobOffload.ts` L103 to L127), so `image_url` data URLs stay inline. It also does nothing on a `StateBackend` (`middleware/fs.ts` L2276 to L2281). One more thing that may help: `createDeepAgent` doesn't pass that option to its filesystem middleware (`agent.ts` L318 to L322 and L491 to L495). But a custom middleware with the same name replaces the default one (`middleware/utils.ts` L47 to L72), so something like this worked in a quick test for me:

```ts
const backend = new CompositeBackend(new StateBackend(), {
  "/large_tool_results/": new StoreBackend(),
  "/blobs/": new StoreBackend(),
});
createDeepAgent({
  backend,
  middleware: [createFilesystemMiddleware({ backend, offloadBinaryContent: true })],
});
```

Large tool results are written through `backend.write("/large_tool_results/...")` (`fs.ts` L2405 to L2407), so that route keeps them out of checkpoints. Declarative subagents build their own stack, so I think each spec would need the same middleware in its `middleware` list. Does that match what you saw, or did you hit something else?

Hey @mrjk05, hope you're doing well! I was curious about this too, so I traced it on current main (b3bf0e4). From what I can see, the leak part is already handled: `setupInputAnalysis()` closes the previous `inputAudioContext` before creating a new one ([webAudioAdapter.ts#L55-L58](https://github.com/elevenlabs/packages/blob/b3bf0e4/packages/client/src/platform/web/webAudioAdapter.ts#L55-L58)), and `setupOutputAnalysis()` does the same for `audioCaptureContext` ([#L82-L85](https://github.com/elevenlabs/packages/blob/b3bf0e4/packages/client/src/platform/web/webAudioAdapter.ts#L82-L85)). So when `TrackSubscribed` fires again after a reconnect and `setupAudioCapture()` runs, the old context gets closed instead of left running.

As I read it, what's still open is the nicer part of @kraenhansen's proposal: keeping one context alive so a reconnect skips reloading the raw audio worklet.

@mrjk05, are you still seeing contexts pile up on a recent version? If so, the version you're on would really help narrow it down.

@kraenhansen, thanks for writing this up so clearly. Would you like to keep this open for the reuse refactor, or is closing on each setup good enough for now? Happy to put up a small PR for the reuse version if that's useful.

Hey @BlueskyFR, hope you're doing well! Thanks for raising this, a fully local setup is a really fair need.

From reading current main, I think it's possible today without any Cloud dependency. The `noiseCancellation` input option takes either the Cloud options or any `FrameProcessor<AudioFrame>` from `@livekit/rtc-node` (`agents/src/voice/room_io/room_io.ts` line 82). When it's a `FrameProcessor`, the room input hands it straight to `AudioStream`, which calls `process(frame)` on every incoming frame of the user's track (`agents/src/voice/room_io/_input.ts` lines 67 and 355 to 363). So you could wrap a local denoiser, for example RNNoise compiled to WASM, in your own class:

```ts
import { AudioFrame, FrameProcessor } from '@livekit/rtc-node';

class LocalDenoiser extends FrameProcessor<AudioFrame> {
  #enabled = true;
  isEnabled() { return this.#enabled; }
  setEnabled(enabled: boolean) { this.#enabled = enabled; }
  process(frame: AudioFrame): AudioFrame {
    const cleaned = denoise(frame.data, frame.sampleRate);
    return new AudioFrame(cleaned, frame.sampleRate, frame.channels, frame.samplesPerChannel);
  }
  close() {}
}

await session.start({
  agent,
  room: ctx.room,
  inputOptions: { noiseCancellation: new LocalDenoiser(), audioSampleRate: 48000 },
});
```

A few things I noticed while tracing it:

1. `process()` is synchronous, so the denoiser has to run inline per frame. If it throws, the original frame is passed through with a warning, so a bug there won't drop audio.
2. Input audio defaults to 24 kHz mono (`room_io.ts` line 126). RNNoise expects 48 kHz audio in 480 sample (10 ms) frames, so setting `audioSampleRate: 48000` saves a resample. The incoming frame length may not line up with 480 samples, so a small carry over buffer might be needed.
3. The session owns the processor and calls `close()` when the input closes, so that's a good place to free WASM memory.
4. If you control the client too, the browser's own `noiseSuppression` capture constraint is another fully local option, though it's much lighter than a model.

Would something like this work for your setup? And would the maintainers find a small custom processor example in `examples/` useful? Happy to put one up if so.

issue.body:
**Describe the bug**

When Ollama returns an assistant message that has both `content` and `tool_calls`, the JS ollama plugin drops the text, both when parsing the response and when sending that turn back on the next request.

`parseMessage` and `toOllamaRequest` in `js/plugins/ollama/src/index.ts` translate between Ollama chat messages and Genkit messages, so a model turn should survive the round trip with all of its parts.

What happens today:

1. The model replies with `{"role":"assistant","content":"Let me check the weather.","tool_calls":[...]}`.
2. `parseMessage` (around L351 to L356) sees non empty `tool_calls` and returns only the `toGenkitToolRequest(...)` parts, so `response.message.content` is never added. Streamed chunks go through `parseMessage` too, so a chunk carrying both text and tool calls loses its text as well.
3. Genkit runs the tool and builds the next request.
4. `toOllamaRequest` (L452) sets `content: toolRequests.length > 0 ? '' : messageText`, so even a model turn that does have a text part is replayed with empty text.
5. The model never sees its own earlier text, and the user never gets it either.

**Expected behavior**

The model turn keeps both parts, `[{ text: 'Let me check the weather.' }, { toolRequest: ... }]`, and the replayed assistant message sends `content: 'Let me check the weather.'` together with `tool_calls`. That matches what the Go plugin already does (`go/plugins/ollama/ollama.go`, around L886 to L888 and L932 to L946).

**To Reproduce**

Checked on main at 5c895679e09810a28744aa1186ef129e5b4eaab6 (genkitx-ollama 1.44.0), with `fetch` stubbed so no Ollama server is needed:

```ts
import { genkit, z } from "genkit";
import { ollama } from "genkitx-ollama";

const sent: any[] = [];
let call = 0;
globalThis.fetch = (async (_url: any, init: any) => {
  sent.push(JSON.parse(init.body));
  call++;
  const message =
    call === 1
      ? {
          role: "assistant",
          content: "Let me check the weather.",
          tool_calls: [
            { function: { name: "weather", arguments: { city: "Paris" } } },
          ],
        }
      : { role: "assistant", content: "Sunny." };
  return new Response(JSON.stringify({ model: "m", message, done: true }));
}) as any;

const ai = genkit({
  plugins: [
    ollama({
      serverAddress: "http://stub",
      models: [{ name: "m", type: "chat", supports: { tools: true } }],
    }),
  ],
});
const weather = ai.defineTool(
  {
    name: "weather",
    description: "w",
    inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),
    outputSchema: z.string(),
  },
  async () => "sunny",
);

const res = await ai.generate({
  model: "ollama/m",
  prompt: "weather?",
  tools: [weather],
});
const modelTurn = res.messages.find(
  (m) => m.role === "model" && m.content.some((p) => p.toolRequest),
);
console.log("model turn:", JSON.stringify(modelTurn?.content));
console.log(
  "replayed:",
  JSON.stringify(sent[1].messages.find((m: any) => m.role === "assistant")),
);
```

Output:

```
model turn: [{"toolRequest":{"name":"weather","input":{"city":"Paris"}}}]
replayed: {"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[...]}
```

Passing a history whose model turn already has `{ text }` plus `{ toolRequest }` straight into `ai.generate({ messages })` shows the request side on its own: the outgoing assistant message still has `content: ""`.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A["Ollama reply: content + tool_calls"] --> B["parseMessage: only toolRequest parts"]
  B --> C["Genkit model turn, text lost"]
  C --> D["toOllamaRequest L452: content ''"]
  D --> E["next request, text lost again"]
```

**Runtime (please complete the following information):**

- OS: MacOS
- Version: `genkit` and `genkitx-ollama` 1.44.0 (main 5c89567)

** Node version

- v24.13.0

**Additional context**

This looks like the same thing #6509 reported for `compat-oai`. @hkarmoush fixed it really cleanly there in #6792, keeping text and tool calls in both directions, and Python did the same in #6282. Thank you for that, it made this one easy to spot.

I also saw @OsamaAnsar's #6782, which is a nice fix for the streaming accumulator in this same file. As far as I can tell it doesn't touch `parseMessage` or L452, so this feels separate but closely related, and I didn't want to step on that work.

A couple of questions before anyone picks it up:

- Would you like the fix to mirror #6792 here, so `parseMessage` keeps a `{ text }` part next to the tool requests and `toOllamaRequest` sends `messageText` alongside `tool_calls`?
- Since #6782 is in the same file, would you rather this land after it, or be folded into it if @OsamaAnsar is up for that? Happy to go whichever way is easiest for everyone, and glad to put up a small PR with tests if that helps.

