issue.body:
### Bug Description

In Google Chat, when someone mentions a user whose display name starts with the bot's `userName` (like `@Bot Dev` when the bot is `Bot`), `onNewMention` fires even though the bot wasn't mentioned.

For context, `message.isMention` is what decides whether a message goes to `onNewMention`, and adapters like Slack, Discord and Telegram set it from platform data. The Google Chat adapter never sets it, so the core falls back to matching `@userName` in the text. That fallback can't tell `@Bot` apart from `@Bot Dev`.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Create a `Chat` with the gchat adapter, `userName: "Bot"` and `botUserId: "users/111"`.
2. A person posts `@Bot Dev can you look at this`. The message has one `USER_MENTION` annotation, for `users/222`.
3. `parseGoogleChatMessage` (webhook, `packages/adapter-gchat/src/index.ts:1442-1494`) and `parsePubSubMessage` (Pub/Sub, `:1247-1287`) both build the message without `isMention`.
4. `normalizeBotMentions` (`:2747-2790`) already compares each annotation's user with `botUserId` (`:2758`), but only uses that to rewrite the text.
5. Since `isMention` is undefined, `setMentionFlags` (`packages/chat/src/chat.ts:3535-3536`) calls `detectMention` (`:3626-3637`). Its pattern `(?<!\w)@Bot(?![\w-])` (case insensitive) matches the start of `@Bot Dev`.
6. `chat.ts:3479` routes the message to `onNewMention` with `isMention: true`.

### Expected Behavior

With `botUserId` set, only a `USER_MENTION` annotation whose user is `botUserId` should count as a mention. `@Bot Dev` (`users/222`) shouldn't fire `onNewMention`, and in subscribed threads `isMention` should be `false`.

The docs read that way too: `gchat.mdx:92` and `:159` say `botUserId` is needed for exact mention detection.

### Actual Behavior

`onNewMention` fires with `isMention: true`. Same result when `users/222` is another bot, and when `botUserId` is unset. A real `@Bot` annotation for `users/111` fires once, as it should.

Webhooks usually only deliver messages that mention the app, so I think the main exposure is the Pub/Sub path for subscribed spaces. There, `isMention` comes out wrong in subscribed threads too.

### Code Sample

```typescript
import { createGoogleChatAdapter } from "@chat-adapter/gchat";
import { createMemoryState } from "@chat-adapter/state-memory";
import { Chat } from "chat";

const gchat = createGoogleChatAdapter({
  credentials,
  userName: "Bot",
  botUserId: "users/111",
});
const chat = new Chat({
  userName: "Bot",
  adapters: { gchat },
  state: createMemoryState(),
});

chat.onNewMention(async (_thread, message) => {
  console.log("mention", message.text, message.isMention);
});

// incoming message
// text: "@Bot Dev can you look at this"
// annotations: [{
//   type: "USER_MENTION",
//   startIndex: 0,
//   length: 8,
//   userMention: {
//     user: { name: "users/222", displayName: "Bot Dev", type: "HUMAN" },
//     type: "MENTION",
//   },
// }]
//
// logs: mention "@Bot Dev can you look at this" true
```

