Hi @MichaelDoyle, hope you're doing well! The layer rules you wrote up in #6563 are really clear, so I tried encoding them for the P0 item. One heads up first: eslint-plugin-boundaries is on 7.x now, and the older `element-types` rule is deprecated in favor of `boundaries/dependencies` with `policies`, so a lot of examples online look different.

I ran this against a small fixture laid out like the proposed `src/app` tree, using the `@core`, `@shared`, `@features` and `@shell` aliases. It flagged exactly the four bad imports I planted (one feature importing another, `traces` importing `runners/shared`, `shared/ui` importing `shared/domain`, `core` importing `shared/ui`) and nothing else, including the lazy `import()` calls in `app.routes.ts`. It sits next to the existing angular-eslint config, which already supplies the TypeScript parser:

```js
import boundaries from "eslint-plugin-boundaries";

const base = ["shared-domain", "shared-ui", "shared-util", "core"];

export default [
  {
    files: ["src/**/*.ts"],
    plugins: { boundaries },
    settings: {
      "import/resolver": { typescript: { project: "./tsconfig.json" } },
      "boundaries/elements": [
        { type: "runners-shared", pattern: "src/app/features/runners/shared" },
        { type: "feature", pattern: "src/app/features/*", capture: ["name"] },
        { type: "shell", pattern: "src/app/shell" },
        { type: "shared-domain", pattern: "src/app/shared/domain" },
        { type: "shared-ui", pattern: "src/app/shared/ui" },
        {
          type: "shared-util",
          pattern: "src/app/shared/{utils,pipes,directives}",
        },
        { type: "core", pattern: "src/app/core" },
        { type: "app", pattern: "src/app" },
      ],
    },
    rules: {
      "boundaries/no-unknown-files": "error",
      "boundaries/dependencies": [
        "error",
        {
          default: "disallow",
          policies: [
            {
              from: { element: { type: "app" } },
              allow: {
                to: { element: { types: ["feature", "shell", ...base] } },
              },
            },
            {
              from: {
                element: { types: ["shell", "feature", "runners-shared"] },
              },
              allow: { to: { element: { types: base } } },
            },
            {
              from: {
                element: { type: "feature", captured: { name: "runners" } },
              },
              allow: { to: { element: { type: "runners-shared" } } },
            },
            {
              from: { element: { type: "shared-domain" } },
              allow: {
                to: {
                  element: { types: ["shared-ui", "shared-util", "core"] },
                },
              },
            },
            {
              from: { element: { type: "shared-ui" } },
              allow: { to: { element: { types: ["shared-util", "core"] } } },
            },
            {
              from: { element: { types: ["shared-util", "core"] } },
              allow: { to: { element: { types: ["shared-util", "core"] } } },
            },
          ],
        },
      ],
    },
  },
];
```

Two things that bit me and fail silently, so they might be worth a comment in the config:

- The `import/resolver` setting (it needs `eslint-import-resolver-typescript`). Without it every alias import is treated as an external package and passes, and all four violations disappeared. Since #6563 moves cross layer imports onto aliases, this one is easy to miss.
- Element order. The first matching pattern wins, so `runners/shared` has to sit above `features/*`, and the catch all `app` (for root files like `app.routes.ts`) has to be last. When I moved `app` to the top, every file classified as `app` and the run came back with zero errors.

I also let shell and features import `shared/{utils,pipes,directives}`, and let `core` use them, since rule 4 allows that for `shared/ui`. Is that what you had in mind, or should those utils move under `core`? Happy to put this up as a PR if the Dev UI source is somewhere I can reach, or it can just live here as a starting point.

issue.body:
### Describe the feature

Coming from https://github.com/nuxt/nuxt/discussions/36731.

Some modules decide *which* dependencies to install, and how to configure them, based on their own options. With `installModule` that was easy, because `setup(options, nuxt)` already has the resolved options:

```ts
export default defineNuxtModule({
  meta: { name: 'my-module', configKey: 'myModule' },
  defaults: { colorMode: true, defaultTheme: 'dark' },
  async setup(options, nuxt) {
    if (!options.colorMode) return
    await installModule('@nuxtjs/color-mode', {
      preference: options.defaultTheme,
      storage: nuxt.options.ssr ? 'cookie' : 'localStorage',
    })
  },
})
```

`installModule` is deprecated now, and `moduleDependencies(nuxt)` only gets `nuxt`. Reading `nuxt.options.myModule` there misses defaults (including function defaults), schema processing, and inline options from `modules: [['my-module', { colorMode: false }]]`. So the same module can end up installing a dependency the user turned off.

Something along these lines is what we'd like to write:

```ts
export default defineNuxtModule({
  meta: { name: 'my-module', configKey: 'myModule' },
  defaults: { colorMode: true, defaultTheme: 'dark' },
  moduleDependencies(nuxt, options) {
    if (!options.colorMode) return {}
    return {
      '@nuxtjs/color-mode': {
        defaults: { preference: options.defaultTheme },
      },
    }
  },
})
```

I saw #34936 and the point that options aren't fully resolved yet at that stage because other modules can still change them. Totally fair. Maybe a narrower version works though: options resolved from user config, inline options, defaults and schema only, without anything other modules push in later. That's what `getOptions(inlineOptions, nuxt)` already computes, and the only missing piece from inside `moduleDependencies` is the inline options. The current workaround is to scan `nuxt.options._layers[].config.modules` for your own entry and call `getOptions` yourself, which works on 4.6.2 but leans on internals.

Open question for the team: what shape would you prefer here? Some options I can think of:

1. pass those partially resolved options (or the inline options) as a second argument to `moduleDependencies`
2. keep `installModule` around (undeprecated) for this specific conditional case
3. some other supported way to declare a dependency as conditional

Happy to go with whatever fits the module system best, mostly want to make sure there's a path for this before `installModule` goes away.

### Additional information

- [x] Would you be willing to help implement this feature?
- [ ] Could this feature be implemented as a module?

### Final checks

- [x] Read the [contribution guide](https://nuxt.com/docs/4.x/community/contribution).
- [x] Check existing [discussions](https://github.com/nuxt/nuxt/discussions) and [issues](https://github.com/nuxt/nuxt/issues).

comment.body:
Thanks for flagging this! It does overlap with #34936, and I linked it in the description because Daniel's point there about options not being fully resolved yet felt really important.

I think this one is a bit narrower though. #34936 was closed as by design. It asked for the final options, which other modules can still change. This one only asks for what `getOptions(inlineOptions, nuxt)` already computes: user config, inline options from `modules: [['my-module', { ... }]]`, defaults and schema, without anything other modules add later. On main, `getModuleDependencies` only receives `nuxt`, while the same loop already has that module's `inlineOptions`. So the inline options and function defaults are the part `nuxt.options` can't cover today.

@danielroe does that narrower version seem workable, or would you rather fold this back into #34936? Totally happy to close this one if it's the same answer, I just wanted a supported path for conditional dependencies before `installModule` goes away. Would love to hear what anyone else thinks too.
